A1. The colored supplement in South Africa. ~ KAFIR
A5. Something the Prince of Wales has been glued to recently. ~ HORSE
A9. Nowadays this is known by the men it keeps. (abbr.) ~ CO
A10. The animals didn't go into the Ark by this. ~ ONES
A12. This belongs in South America. ~ PERU
A13. What your friends do when your liquor gives out. ~ GO
A15. The way of a slow coach. ~ POKEY
A16. What Virginia gets giddy on yet stays sober. ~ REELS
A18. Tho it may get hot this doesn't mind being hit on the head. ~ NAIL
A20. Every harvest this beats it for the farm. ~ FLAIL
A22. Groceries. ~ EATS
A24. What the forty thieves got all boiled up about. ~ OIL
A25. What a good punch is composed of. ~ VIM
A26. This is thrown around in Hawaii. ~ LEI
A27. A regular Greek god. ~ EROS
A29. An intriguing apartment. ~ HAREM
A31. A secret depository. (pl.) ~ PALS
A32. These goods are usually picked up by the fence. ~ STOLEN
A34. How the woman came out from the plastic surgeon's. ~ REMADE
A36. A sheepish answer. ~ BAA
A37. A salt producer. ~ LOT
A38. This goes with women and song. ~ SHERRY
A41. A trench digger. ~ GOPHER
A44. The last house on your visiting list. ~ POOR
A45. A Chinaman's finger nail. ~ TALON
A47. This blows with the wind. ~ SAIL
A49. A successful way to avoid bill collectors. ~ RUN
A50. You'll have to add an ell to make this roomy enough for two. ~ CEL
A51. Baby talk. ~ GOO
A52. No key can lock this door. ~ OPEN
A54. What Robin Hood used to get his man. ~ SHAFT
A56. The low-down on an opera singer. ~ ALTO
A57. What the Dutchman used when he gave them the boot. ~ SABOT
A59. The proper feeling to have for a ghost. ~ EERIE
A61. An abbreviated direction. ~ SO
A63. When these are shelled they don't get a shock. ~ PEAS
A64. This makes the bottles disappear. ~ RAID
A65. Where Robinson Crusoe was put on the spot. (abbr.) ~ IS
A66. What night watchmen do besides their duty. ~ SLEEP
A67. What fish without noses do. ~ SMELL

D1. This'll put you to sleep—temporarily. ~ KO
D2. What Ed Wynn plays besides his roles. ~ FOOL
D3. After making this black and blue it is usually put into the pen. ~ INK
D4. What fishes bark their fins on. ~ REEF
D5. This is frequently down-trodden. ~ HEEL
D6. Here's a raw one for you. ~ ORE
D7. What a prince takes after his father. ~ RULE
D8. For instance. ~ EG
D9. A good paddling is what this needs. ~ CANOE
D11. The kind of retreat that covers a lot of ground. ~ SYLVAN
D12. This is of the first grade. ~ PRIMER
D14. A wet goal. ~ OASIS
D15. Careful, this guy is liable to take you for a ride. ~ PILOT
D17. Greens good golfers thrive on. ~ SALAD
D19. What the newly rich put on for a party. (pl). ~ AIRS
D21. What the coquette gave her boy friend for Xmas. ~ AIR
D23. By providing your own vision this will enable you to see and hear things at quite a distance. ~ TELE
D28. A serious condition. ~ SOBER
D29. A good pumper. ~ HEART
D30. A colored patch. ~ MELON
D31. These may lead you astray. ~ PATHS
D33. A household god. ~ LAR
D35. This caused a clean-up at the racetrack office. ~ MOP
D38. A food liquidation. ~ SOUP
D39. These are useful in getting an edge on. ~ HONES
D40. A good place for a wet party. (pl.) ~ YACHTS
D41. The man that prefers small tee parties. ~ GOLFER
D42. This is worth ten dollars. ~ EAGLE
D43. What takes place at the bargain counter. ~ RIOT
D44. What an actor just can't do without. ~ PROPS
D46. Where the cows come home from. ~ LEA
D48. A fortune teller can't get these back when they're lost. ~ LOOKS
D53. You get this in the neck. ~ NAPE
D54. This makes the worst criminal come clean. ~ SOAP
D55. Just an old English custom. (pl.) ~ TEAS
D56. An important fellow in de camp. ~ AIDE
D58. Don't sit down on this—you'll only have to get right up again. ~ BEE
D60. What the motorist came home on. ~ RIM
D62. This is what the contention was over. ~ OS
D65. This stands for a man in France. ~ IL


